window.docContents[240].resolve({"/Equiv/#Hoare-Logic--Equiv___-Program-Equivalence--Program-Transformation":{"contents":"A program transformation is a function that takes a program as input\nand produces a modified program as output.  Compiler\noptimizations such as constant folding are canonical examples,\nbut there are many others.A program transformation is sound if it preserves the\nbehavior of the original program.  Since Aexp, Bexp, and\nCom all have ≃-equivalence via the Equiv class, we can state\nsoundness once, generically, instead of writing it out separately for each type of Imp syntax.\n\ndef TransSound {T : Type} [Equiv T] (trans : T → T) : Prop :=\n  ∀ (t : T), (trans t) ≃ t\n\n@[simp]\ntheorem transSound_def {T : Type} [Equiv T] {trans : T → T} :\n    TransSound trans ↔ ∀ (t : T), (trans t) ≃ t := by rfl\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Hoare Logic\u0009Equiv: Program Equivalence","header":"4.3. Program Transformation","id":"/Equiv/#Hoare-Logic--Equiv___-Program-Equivalence--Program-Transformation"}});